-- title:
--   The Huber function vanishes only at the origin
-- statement:
--   For $c > 0$, the Huber function vanishes only at the origin: if $H_c(t) = 0$ then $t = 0$. With
--
--   $$H_c(t) = \begin{cases} t^2/2, & |t| \le c,\\[2pt] c|t| - c^2/2, & |t| \ge c,\end{cases} \qquad H_c(t) = 0 \;\Longrightarrow\; t = 0,$$
--
--   the quadratic branch forces $t^2 = 0$ and hence $t = 0$, while on the linear branch $|t| \ge c$ gives $c|t| - c^2/2 \ge c^2/2 > 0$, so no root exists there. The two branches agree at $|t| = c$, so the case distinction is exhaustive.
-- source:
--   《有理动量 Nesterov 紧界与半径归一化五算法共同最坏情形 — 可审计完整证明稿》 (manuscript, 2026-10-08), §25, eq. (25.2); Lean: FullFormalization.LiftedHuber lines 118-127

